  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 AM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nSplit midlevel flow over the CONUS continues to periodically punt \nwaves into the Great Lakes Region this weekend. Surface low pressure \npositioned over central Illinois this morning will continue to \nspread moisture across the northwoods with subsequent showers and \nstorms through this evening. Clipper system finally begins to move \ndownstream Sunday morning as subsidence aloft begins to dry out the \nlow levels. Conditions remain dry with breezy post frontal winds \nthis Sunday and climatologically cool temperatures.\n\nTypical Northern Michigan weather persists the remainder of the \nforecast period as the downstream 500mb ridging pattern currently \nover the central/southwestern U.S. expanding northward through the \nmidwest. Subsequent high pressure and benign weather will persist \nthrough the midweek. The next chance of precipitation returns on \nWednesday as large-scale closed low pressure over Hudson Bay extends \na troughing regime into the high plains and pivots waves of energy \nacross the Great Lakes Region through the end of the week. Long-\nrange ensembles depict just enough moisture and instability to \ncreate showers and storms, but the likelihood for widespread heavy \nrainfall or severe weather remains low.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nToday and Sunday: Ongoing showers will persist today through this \nevening as surface low pressure moves to the Northeastern U.S. Some \nhigher instability will create scattered embedded convection this \nafternoon. Combined with PWATs over an inch and a half, this will \nmaintain the risk for localized heavy downpours, particularly in \nEastern Upper and some parts of Northern Lower. Subsidence will \nbuild aloft this Sunday and begin to dry out profiles across the \nCWA. Breezy post-frontal winds and temperatures in the 70s will \ncreate conditions feel like early fall rather than early \n\nMonday and Tuesday: Midlevel ridging over the central U.S. begins to \nbuild across the midwest, returning typical Northern Michigan summer \nconditions. Mostly sunny skies will persist with light winds as \ntemperatures gradually recover back to climatologically normal. \nSubsidence aloft will support surface high pressure and no \nprecipitation this Monday and Tuesday.\n\nWednesday through Friday Outlook: Midlevel troughing currently off \nthe Gulf of Alaska will progress eastward and develop large scale \nlow pressure over Hudson Bay. Embedded height waves will pivot \nacross the midwest the remainder of the forecast period resulting in \nperiods of showers and storms beyond the midweek. The best chance of \nrain remains on Wednesday as a disheveled cold front sweeps across \nthe midwest. Current models depict moisture being cut off from the \nsouth and relatively weak forcing leading to only light QPF amounts \nand scattered storms. Low chances of rain remain in the forecast \nThursday and Friday as afternoon heating and instability will likely \ncreate convection across the region, but again, no heavy rainfall or \nstrong storms are expected at this time.",
